#{[{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046645} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186531703} :activity/id #uuid "68138858-7af6-59b5-82a5-7a64f2b7d918" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048694 :topic/id #uuid "b52b86c6-5073-5d60-a17c-32a308489156" :topic/math-academy-id 2638} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186531662 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186364406 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bc4fe3ab-834d-54e6-8aa2-1f24a32b9e3b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\pi +1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364408} {:answer/id #uuid "11e0dcd6-b288-598a-ba1c-229ef1490ba0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi -1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364409} {:answer/id #uuid "beebdd75-a401-5283-81ff-5aa849295dce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi -2}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364410} {:answer/id #uuid "377ccde4-480f-5a52-99b4-878b8324b114" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi +2}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364411} {:answer/id #uuid "ef48135e-5181-5bd2-954a-39061d970853" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\pi +2}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364412}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186364411} :answer-field/id #uuid "49a9a65b-096a-5622-a126-8ea061e266fb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364407}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "088ac295-32da-54ad-8397-ade18f5cdf50" :question/math-academy-id "q-76683" :question/problem "Evaluate the integral ${\\int }_{C}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}s$, where the curve $C$ is given in polar form by $r=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,$ for $-\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\le \\theta \\le 0.$" :question/worked-solution "In polar coordinates, the formula for the line integral is given by\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe connection between polar and Cartesian coordinates gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta , \\\\ y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, along our curve, we have $x={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta $ and $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $, where $-\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\le \\theta \\le 0.$ \n\nNow, if $f(x,y)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ) & ={({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta )}^{2}+{(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )}^{2} \\\\  & ={cos}^{4}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta ({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta ) \\\\  & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta } & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}\\theta }(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ) \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  & =\\sqrt{{(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )}^{2}+{(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta )}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta } \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we now evaluate the integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /4}^{0}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /4}^{0}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\cdot 1\\cdot \\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /4}^{0}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /4}^{0}\\frac{1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta }{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta }{4}{|}_{-\\pi /4}^{0} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(0-(-\\frac{\\pi }{2})-(-1)) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi +2}{8}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "007eb044-32d9-58ff-9c52-c7f147890537" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186531697}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186531697 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186364413 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "49adc8e9-1dc5-5cb5-9175-f91e269efe35" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "192" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364415} {:answer/id #uuid "e94aff50-1cf2-58b3-85e2-e76471a133a5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "96" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364416} {:answer/id #uuid "7fdf46cb-8dea-538a-974a-83bf0db567f5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "256" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364417} {:answer/id #uuid "119dacc9-0e3b-5285-87d0-21d3cc26e860" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "512" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364418} {:answer/id #uuid "bddd3fe2-39af-5dc8-a534-e22068d4a73c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "384" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364419}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186364415} :answer-field/id #uuid "7944764b-8f2e-517b-9311-deccaafc6156" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364414}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "13c81ebd-eba4-55aa-a40f-bfa1b49cc9df" :question/math-academy-id "q-76763" :question/problem "Evaluate the integral of the function $f(x,y)={x}^{3}y$ along the polar curve $r=4$ for $0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{4}.$" :question/worked-solution "In polar coordinates, the formula for the line integral is given by\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe connection between polar and Cartesian coordinates gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta =4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta , \\\\ y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta =4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, along our curve, we have $x=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ and $y=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $, where $0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{4}.$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)={x}^{3}y$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ) & =(4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{3}4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =256{cos}^{3}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta } & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}\\theta }(4)=0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  & =\\sqrt{{4}^{2}+{0}^{2}} \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}{x}^{3}y\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}256{cos}^{3}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\cdot 4\\cdot \\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =1\\,024{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}{cos}^{3}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this using the substitution $u=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,$ $\\text{d}u=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta $ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1\\,024{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}{cos}^{3}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta  & =-1\\,024{\\int }_{1}^{\\sqrt{2}/2}{u}^{3}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-1\\,024\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}{u}^{4}\\,{|}_{1}^{\\sqrt{2}/2} \\\\  & =-256((\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})^{4}-{1}^{4}) \\\\  & =-256(-\\frac{3}{4}) \\\\  & =192\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "87361f89-76de-51cb-bf0f-80295869b0f0"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186531699 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186364399 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3e48e2f8-a998-591f-85a7-74216c330826" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364401} {:answer/id #uuid "86c854f3-9b37-56bf-8ed0-d656aca9cb87" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364402} {:answer/id #uuid "b16fdea2-eae9-5267-a3ec-033d8b940279" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364403} {:answer/id #uuid "828d078c-1cec-5c53-b243-49495f238d47" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364404} {:answer/id #uuid "d2a1bb85-3d86-5ec5-aae3-1f0c60397c3a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364405}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186364402} :answer-field/id #uuid "5e304ce4-5b90-51ac-8564-dc2bcdec4f03" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364400}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "3efc6eb6-1183-58cd-bd1a-3a29f39410eb" :question/math-academy-id "q-76614" :question/problem "Evaluate the integral of the function $f(x,y)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}$ along the polar curve $r=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,$ for $0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "In polar coordinates, the formula for the line integral is given by\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe connection between polar and Cartesian coordinates gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta , \\\\ y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, along our curve, we have $x=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ and $y={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta $, where $0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}.$ \n\nNow, since $f(x,y)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ) & =(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+({sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )^{2} \\\\  & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{4}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta ({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta ) \\\\  & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(\\theta ) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}\\theta }(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta } & =\\sqrt{(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, we now evaluate the integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\cdot 1\\cdot \\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\frac{1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta }{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\theta -\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta }{4}\\,{|}_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi -0}{4}-\\frac{0-0}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "8f914621-4d05-5aa5-9db2-878a4bb76393" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186531662}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186531703 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152741 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "88df318e-7cef-5a1b-beb4-ebd43d0a2b69" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "9(e-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343422} {:answer/id #uuid "9018c26a-96ea-5a45-9910-5c15a84e4b04" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3({e}^{\\pi /2}-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343496} {:answer/id #uuid "a12e6b2b-2c46-5a38-94d1-fac97bd2c7fc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "9({e}^{2}-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343659} {:answer/id #uuid "c1f6766e-d792-5a01-b94e-c00eb881526d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3({e}^{3}-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343943} {:answer/id #uuid "edcaf3b2-c406-5e3f-b708-5782842800a0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3{e}^{\\pi /2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344328}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186343943} :answer-field/id #uuid "b1ba1fde-9f77-5ff0-a0e1-ed2ea5ee8d8b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344804}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "55dd4f9a-c59d-565d-9412-8e04ab857f89" :question/math-academy-id "q-76347" :question/problem "Evaluate the integral of the function $f(x,y)=x{e}^{y}$ along the polar curve $r=3$ for $0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "In polar coordinates, the formula for the line integral is given by\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe connection between polar and Cartesian coordinates gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta =3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta , \\\\ y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta =3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, along our curve, we have $x=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ and $y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $, where $0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}.$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=x{e}^{y}$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ) & =3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,{e}^{3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta } & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}\\theta }(3)=0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  & =\\sqrt{{3}^{2}+{0}^{2}} \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}x{e}^{y}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,{e}^{3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }\\cdot 3\\cdot \\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =3{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,{e}^{3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this using the substitution $u=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,$ $\\text{d}u=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta $ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,{e}^{3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  & =3{\\int }_{0}^{3}{e}^{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =3{e}^{u}{|}_{0}^{3} \\\\  & =3({e}^{3}-{e}^{0}) \\\\  & =3({e}^{3}-1)\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "a457e0db-1973-5b6b-8d71-9a6a062a4f73" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186531725}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186531725 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186455508 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "082112eb-8a26-5265-b137-26bd8c295b06" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{sin}^{3}⁡\\theta \\,{(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )}^{2}\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454924} {:answer/id #uuid "0ae41ca2-e608-5696-b440-d41c065df75e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,{(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )}^{2}\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454928} {:answer/id #uuid "5cbc00c8-62ae-5a6f-8037-f55f4dd09a38" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,{(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )}^{2}\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186455070} {:answer/id #uuid "76d63731-dd9b-510e-8131-e402a45ba6a7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,{(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )}^{2}\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186455120} {:answer/id #uuid "de572935-367e-52cb-8f52-f59649494fbd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,{(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )}^{2}\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186455287}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186455070} :answer-field/id #uuid "436cc0aa-bff3-5b92-a639-ba2e62b0da93" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186455360}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "ee634955-812e-57a4-9691-da172190ae0a" :question/math-academy-id "q-76611" :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to the integral ${\\int }_{C}(x+y)^{2}\\,\\text{d}s,$ where $C$ is the section of the polar curve $r=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,$ for $0\\le \\theta \\le \\pi ?$" :question/worked-solution "In polar coordinates, the formula for the line integral is given by\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe connection between polar and Cartesian coordinates gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta , \\\\ y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, along our curve, we have $x={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta $ and $y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $, where $0\\le \\theta \\le \\pi .$\n\nNow, if $f(x,y)=(x+y)^{2}$, we obtain  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ) & ={({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )}^{2} \\\\  & ={(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta (\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ))}^{2} \\\\  & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,{(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNext, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta } & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}\\theta }(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  & =\\sqrt{{(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )}^{2}+{(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta } \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}(x+y)^{2}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,{(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )}^{2}\\cdot 1\\cdot \\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,{(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )}^{2}\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "eb57df81-a928-539a-b8d1-a80b4295d9a1" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186531699}}] :activity/title "Line Integrals of Scalar Functions Over Polar Curves" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530857}]}
